2026-06-30 15:23:59,222 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-7c5347e0-3a46-4ba3-ab5c-65cba18bd4d6', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert SDRF-Proteomics annotator for crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nExtract the following SDRF metadata terms from the provided publication text and/or PRIDE project description.\nFor each term, return the best available ontology-resolved value with its accession where indicated.\n\nTERMS TO EXTRACT:\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nterm: organism\nsdrf_column: characteristics[organism]\nontology: NCBITaxon (e.g. NCBITaxon:9606)\ndescription: Source species of the proteome of interest in the biological samples analyzed by mass spectrometry. Return the scientific name. \nSource species maybe different than host species when proteins from one organism are expressed in another. \ninstructions: Use NCBITaxon accession. Common examples: Homo sapiens (NCBITaxon:9606),\n  Mus musculus (NCBITaxon:10090), Saccharomyces cerevisiae (NCBITaxon:4932),\n  Escherichia coli (NCBITaxon:562). If multiple species, return a list.\n\nterm: organism part\nsdrf_column: characteristics[organism part]\nontology: UBERON or BTO\ndescription: Main normalized anatomical term for the sample analyzed by mass spectrometry. \ninstructions: Use the clearest ontology-backed anatomy term. Examples: whole-cell lysate, mitochondria,\n  nucleus, cytoplasm, liver, heart, brain, cell, ect. Return null if not applicable\n  (e.g. purified recombinant proteins with no tissue origin).\n\nterm: cell line\nsdrf_column: characteristics[cell line]\nontology: CLO, BTO, or EFO (e.g. CLO:0001230 for HEK293)\ndescription: Name of the cell line if a cell line was used.\ninstructions: Return null if no cell line was used (primary cells or tissues).\n\nterm: cell type\nsdrf_column: characteristics[cell type]\nontology: CL, BTO, or CLO\ndescription: Cell type of the sample.\ninstructions: Return null if not applicable or not stated.\n\nterm: material type\nsdrf_column: characteristics[material type]\nontology: PRIDE:0000837\ndescription: Type of biological material analyzed. Differentiates whether the whole cell lysate was analyzed, just a specific anatomical structure or tissue, or if the samples were purified for specific proteins before mass spectrometry analysis. \nallowed_values: tissue, whole cell lysate, organell, membrane, synthetic, purified proteins, affinity captured\ninstructions: Choose the best matching fixed value. Return null if unclear.\n\nterm: strain or breed\nsdrf_column: characteristics[strain or breed]\nontology: free text\ndescription: Strain, breed, cultivar, or ecotype of the organism.\ninstructions: Return null if not stated.\n\nterm: developmental stage\nsdrf_column: characteristics[developmental stage]\nontology: EFO\ndescription: Developmental stage of the organism or donor.\ninstructions: Return null if not stated or not applicable.\n\nterm: technology type\nsdrf_column: technology type\nontology: PRIDE:0000663\ndescription: Type of technology used. For XL-MS this is always fixed.\nallowed_values: proteomic profiling by mass spectrometry\ninstructions: Always return "proteomic profiling by mass spectrometry" for XL-MS datasets.\n\nterm: source name\nsdrf_column: source name\nontology: PRIDE:0000623\ndescription: Unique identifier for the biological sample. Typically derived from\n  the dataset file names or sample descriptions in the paper.\ninstructions: Describe the naming convention used (e.g. "PXD_stem" or "condition_replicate").\n  This is auto-generated from file names; return a description of the sample naming pattern only.\n\nterm: assay name\nsdrf_column: assay name\nontology: NCIT:C178857\ndescription: Unique identifier for each MS run/assay. Typically the raw file stem.\ninstructions: Describe the naming convention. This is auto-generated; return the pattern only.\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nReturn a single JSON object where each key is the exact sdrf_column string shown above.\nFor each key, return a dict with:\n  "value": the extracted value (string, list, or null)\n  "accession": ontology accession if resolvable (e.g. "NCBITaxon:9606"), else null\n  "confidence": "high", "medium", or "low"\n  "evidence_quote": short verbatim quote from the text supporting the extraction, or null\n  "evidence_location": where in the paper (e.g. "Methods", "Abstract"), or null\n\nExample output structure:\n{\n  "characteristics[organism]": {"value": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae", "accession": "NCBITaxon:4932", "confidence": "high", "evidence_quote": "...", "evidence_location": "Methods"},\n  "characteristics[organism part]": {"value": "mitochondria", "accession": null, "confidence": "high", "evidence_quote": "...", "evidence_location": "Methods"},\n  ...\n}'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '[TITLE]\nProfiling the diversity of agonist-selective effects on the proximal proteome environment of G protein-coupled receptors\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nG protein-coupled receptors (GPCRs) regulate many aspects of physiology and represent actionable targets for drug discovery. Activation of specific signaling pathways downstream of G-protein coupled receptors (GPCRs) or targeting the receptors at selected cellular locations has the potential to provide therapeutic actions with fewer side effects. However, the understanding of the molecular mechanisms underlying GPCR function is limited in the dynamic cellular environment, hampering drug discovery efforts towards selective ligands. Proximity biotin labeling based on an engineered ascorbic acid peroxidase (APEX) combined with quantitative mass spectrometry is a powerful method to delineate these mechanisms given its capacity to simultaneously capture proximal protein interaction networks and the cellular location of the receptor. However, a major challenge is to extract the various information from these complex datasets. Here, we describe a computational framework for proximity labeling datasets which predicts ligand-dependent subcellular location of GPCRs and quantitatively deconvolutes the effect of receptor location and proximal interactors. We applied this approach to the mu-opioid receptor and not only monitored distinct effects of ligands on receptor trafficking, but also discovered two novel regulators, EYA4 and KCTD12, which modulate MOR-driven G protein-dependent signaling.\n\n[METHODS]\nFor the APEX experiments the following method was used to perform biotinylation, enrichment and prepare the samples for MS analysis. 500 μM biotin-phenol was pre-incubated with cells for 30 min at 37°C. 10 μM DAMGO, morphine, or PZM21 was added for the noted period of time. APEX labeling was initiated by addition of 1 mM H2O2. The labeling reaction was allowed to continue for 30 s, media was removed, and the cells were washed three times in ice cold quenching buffer (TBS supplemented with CaCl2, sodium ascorbate, sodium azide, and Trolox). Cells were lysed in RIPA (50 mM Tris, 150 mM NaCl, 1% Triton X-100, 0.5% deoxycholate, 0.1% SDS, pH 7.4) supplemented with 10 mM sodium ascorbate and protease inhibitors (Roche Complete). Samples were sonicated, spun down, and the supernatant was applied to streptavidin agarose resin, and incubated overnight at 4°C. Streptavidin agarose resin was washed two times in RIPA buffer, four times in TBS, one time in 50 mM NH4HCO3, 3 M Urea. Samples were reduced on resin by adding TCEP, followed by alkylation with iodoacetamide. The reaction was quenched upon addition of DTT. The streptavidin agarose resin was spun down and the buffer exchanged to 50 mM NH4HCO3, 2 M Urea. Biotinylated proteins were digested with trypsin overnight at 37°C and desalted for MS analysis. For the AP-MS experiments, HEK293 cells stably expressing mu-opioid receptor were transfected with individual Strep-tagged expression constructs. After more than 38 hours, cells were dissociated at room temperature using PBS supplemented with 10 mM EDTA and subsequently washed with PBS. Cell pellets were frozen on dry ice and stored at −80°C. Frozen cell pellets were thawed on ice and suspended in 1 ml Lysis Buffer [IP Buffer (50 mM Tris-HCl, pH 7.4 at 4°C, 150 mM NaCl, 1 mM EDTA) supplemented with 0.5% Nonidet P 40 Substitute, protease and phosphatase inhibitor cocktails]. Affinity purifications were performed as follows. MagStrep “type3” beads (30 μl per sample) were equilibrated twice with Wash Buffer (IP Buffer supplemented with 0.05% NP40) and incubated with the lysate for 2 hours at 4°C. Following incubation, beads were washed twice with Wash Buffer and with IP Buffer. To directly digest bead-bound proteins, beads were resuspended in Denaturation-Reduction Buffer (2 M urea, 50 mM Tris-HCl pH 8.0, 1 mM DTT). Bead-bound proteins were denatured and reduced at 37°C for 30 minutes and after bringing them to room temperature, alkylated in the dark with 3 mM iodoacetamide for 45 minutes and quenched with 3 mM DTT for 10 minutes. Proteins were then digested with trypsin at 37°C. All steps were performed with constant shaking at 1,100 rpm. Peptides were desalted for MS analysis.\n'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
