(K,nterm; AC=XLMOD:09317), DSPU (K,nterm), DSSBU (K,nterm),\n    DHSO (carbonyl-reactive D,E; AC=XLMOD:02226), BMSO (cysteine-specific; C),\n    SDASO (K,nterm; photo+MS-cleavable), SDASO-L (K,nterm; photo+MS-cleavable),\n    SSD (K,nterm), CBDPS (K,nterm), iQlnk (K,nterm)\n  PIR-type enrichable (reporter-ion, NOT backbone MS-cleavable): PIR (K,nterm), BDP-NHP (K,nterm)\n  Common non-cleavable: BS3/BS2G (K,nterm; AC=XLMOD:02000/02005), DSS (K,nterm; AC=XLMOD:02001), EDC (D,E,nterm; AC=XLMOD:02010), formaldehyde (K,nterm)\n  IMAC-enrichable (non-cleavable): PhoX (K,nterm; AC=XLMOD:02258), pDSPE (K,nterm; AC=null)\n  Photo-crosslinkers (non-cleavable, UV-activated): diazirine (K or incorporated aa), benzophenone (Bpa, pBpa; C or incorporated aa), SDA/sulfo-SDA (K,nterm; AC=XLMOD:02134/02136), aryl azide\n  NOTE: Do NOT extract proximity labeling enzymes (TurboID, BioID, APEX, biotin-phenol+H2O2) as crosslinkers — these are NOT chemical cross-linking reagents.\n\nterm: crosslinker concentration\nsdrf_column: comment[crosslinker concentration]\nontology: PRIDE:0000951\ndescription: Concentration of the crosslinking reagent used in the reaction.\npattern: number + mM or uM (e.g. "1 mM", "500 uM", "0.5 mM")\ninstructions: Return null if not stated.\n\nterm: crosslinker to protein ratio\nsdrf_column: comment[crosslinker to protein ratio]\nontology: PRIDE:0000952\ndescription: Molar ratio of crosslinker to protein in the reaction.\npattern: ratio expression (e.g. "50:1", "100-fold molar excess", "5 mM per 1 mg/mL")\ninstructions: Return null if not stated.\n\nterm: quenching reagent\nsdrf_column: comment[quenching reagent]\nontology: PRIDE:0000953\ndescription: Reagent used to stop, quench, neutralize, terminate, or consume the crosslinking reaction.\ninstructions:\n  Extract only if explicitly stated in the paper, PRIDE metadata, or cited protocol text.\n  Search for synonyms including: quenched, stopped, terminated, neutralized, blocked,\n  residual crosslinker, washed with, incubated with, reaction was halted.\nCommon quenchers:\n  - NHS ester crosslinkers such as BS3, DSS, DSSO, DSBU: Tris, ammonium bicarbonate, glycine, lysine\n  - Formaldehyde crosslinking: glycine or Tris are common, but only annotate if explicitly stated\nFor formaldehyde datasets:\n  - Inspect the 3–5 sentences after the formaldehyde crosslinking sentence.\n  - If the text says "as described previously" or "as described before", and no quencher is named, attempt to read the cited artical and extract the quenching reagent from its methods section.\n    If that fall back failes then return value=null with confidence="low" and reasoning="quenching reagent not explicitly stated in provided text; may require cited protocol."\n  - Do NOT infer glycine solely because formaldehyde was used.\n  - Do NOT treat PBS washes alone as a quenching reagent.\n  - Treat Tris-containing lysis buffer as a possible quencher only if the text clearly implies it was use\n  \nterm: crosslink distance\nsdrf_column: characteristics[crosslink distance]\nontology: PRIDE:0000948\ndescription: Maximum Cα–Cα distance constraint (spacer arm length) provided by the crosslinker.\npattern: number + Å (e.g. "11.4 Å", "30 Å", "~27 Å")\ninstructions: Extract the stated spacer length or max distance. Common values:\n  BS3/DSS ≈ 11.4 Å, DSSO ≈ 10.1 Å, DSBU ≈ 12.5 Å, EDC ≈ 0 Å (zero-length).\n  Return null if not explicitly stated in the text.\n\nterm: chemical cross-linking coupled with ms\nsdrf_column: comment[chemical cross-linking coupled with ms]\nontology: PRIDE:0000430\ndescription: MS-based cross-linking methodology identifier.\nallowed_values: cross-linking mass spectrometry\ninstructions: Always return "cross-linking mass spectrometry" for any XL-MS dataset.\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nReturn a single JSON object where each key is the exact sdrf_column string shown above.\nFor each key, return a dict with:\n  "value": the extracted value (string, list, or null). For comment[cross-linker] the\n           "value" field should contain the full NT=...;AC=...;CL=...;TA=... string,\n           and also include separate sub-fields: "NT", "AC", "CL", "TA".\n  "accession": ontology accession if resolvable, else null\n  "confidence": "high", "medium", or "low"\n  "evidence_quote": short verbatim quote from the text, or null\n  "evidence_location": location in the paper (e.g. "Methods"), or null\n\nExample for comment[cross-linker]:\n{\n  "comment[cross-linker]": {\n    "value": "NT=DSSO;AC=XLMOD:02056;CL=yes;TA=K,S,T,Y",\n    "NT": "DSSO", "AC": "XLMOD:02056", "CL": "yes", "TA": "K,S,T,Y",\n    "confidence": "high", "evidence_quote": "crosslinked with 1 mM DSSO",\n    "evidence_location": "Methods"\n  }\n}'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '[TITLE]\nProfiling the diversity of agonist-selective effects on the proximal proteome environment of G protein-coupled receptors\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nG protein-coupled receptors (GPCRs) regulate many aspects of physiology and represent actionable targets for drug discovery. Activation of specific signaling pathways downstream of G-protein coupled receptors (GPCRs) or targeting the receptors at selected cellular locations has the potential to provide therapeutic actions with fewer side effects. However, the understanding of the molecular mechanisms underlying GPCR function is limited in the dynamic cellular environment, hampering drug discovery efforts towards selective ligands. Proximity biotin labeling based on an engineered ascorbic acid peroxidase (APEX) combined with quantitative mass spectrometry is a powerful method to delineate these mechanisms given its capacity to simultaneously capture proximal protein interaction networks and the cellular location of the receptor. However, a major challenge is to extract the various information from these complex datasets. Here, we describe a computational framework for proximity labeling datasets which predicts ligand-dependent subcellular location of GPCRs and quantitatively deconvolutes the effect of receptor location and proximal interactors. We applied this approach to the mu-opioid receptor and not only monitored distinct effects of ligands on receptor trafficking, but also discovered two novel regulators, EYA4 and KCTD12, which modulate MOR-driven G protein-dependent signaling.\n\n[METHODS]\nFor the APEX experiments the following method was used to perform biotinylation, enrichment and prepare the samples for MS analysis. 500 μM biotin-phenol was pre-incubated with cells for 30 min at 37°C. 10 μM DAMGO, morphine, or PZM21 was added for the noted period of time. APEX labeling was initiated by addition of 1 mM H2O2. The labeling reaction was allowed to continue for 30 s, media was removed, and the cells were washed three times in ice cold quenching buffer (TBS supplemented with CaCl2, sodium ascorbate, sodium azide, and Trolox). Cells were lysed in RIPA (50 mM Tris, 150 mM NaCl, 1% Triton X-100, 0.5% deoxycholate, 0.1% SDS, pH 7.4) supplemented with 10 mM sodium ascorbate and protease inhibitors (Roche Complete). Samples were sonicated, spun down, and the supernatant was applied to streptavidin agarose resin, and incubated overnight at 4°C. Streptavidin agarose resin was washed two times in RIPA buffer, four times in TBS, one time in 50 mM NH4HCO3, 3 M Urea. Samples were reduced on resin by adding TCEP, followed by alkylation with iodoacetamide. The reaction was quenched upon addition of DTT. The streptavidin agarose resin was spun down and the buffer exchanged to 50 mM NH4HCO3, 2 M Urea. Biotinylated proteins were digested with trypsin overnight at 37°C and desalted for MS analysis. For the AP-MS experiments, HEK293 cells stably expressing mu-opioid receptor were transfected with individual Strep-tagged expression constructs. After more than 38 hours, cells were dissociated at room temperature using PBS supplemented with 10 mM EDTA and subsequently washed with PBS. Cell pellets were frozen on dry ice and stored at −80°C. Frozen cell pellets were thawed on ice and suspended in 1 ml Lysis Buffer [IP Buffer (50 mM Tris-HCl, pH 7.4 at 4°C, 150 mM NaCl, 1 mM EDTA) supplemented with 0.5% Nonidet P 40 Substitute, protease and phosphatase inhibitor cocktails]. Affinity purifications were performed as follows. MagStrep “type3” beads (30 μl per sample) were equilibrated twice with Wash Buffer (IP Buffer supplemented with 0.05% NP40) and incubated with the lysate for 2 hours at 4°C. Following incubation, beads were washed twice with Wash Buffer and with IP Buffer. To directly digest bead-bound proteins, beads were resuspended in Denaturation-Reduction Buffer (2 M urea, 50 mM Tris-HCl pH 8.0, 1 mM DTT). Bead-bound proteins were denatured and reduced at 37°C for 30 minutes and after bringing them to room temperature, alkylated in the dark with 3 mM iodoacetamide for 45 minutes and quenched with 3 mM DTT for 10 minutes. Proteins were then digested with trypsin at 37°C. All steps were performed with constant shaking at 1,100 rpm. Peptides were desalted for MS analysis.\n'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
